COUNTY COUNSEL IMPARTIAL ANALYSIS- SAN BERNARDINO MOUNTAINS COMMUNITY
SUBJECT HOSPITAL DISTRICT BOND MEASURE ELECTION TO BE HELD ON NOVEMBER 3, 2026
This Measure was placed on the ballot by a resolution adopted by the Board of Directors of the San
Bernardino Mountains Community Hospital District (District). A "yes" vote by at least two-thirds of the voters
voting on this Measure will authorize the District to issue bonds in the amount of up to $61,000,000 to be
used solely for the purposes set forth in this ballot Measure. A "no" vote means that the District will not be
authorized to issue and sell up to $61,000,000 in bonds set forth in the Measure."
The District's Resolution was adopted pursuant to California Health and Safety Code section 32301 et seq.,
Government Code sections 53506 and 53531 , and Article XIIIA of the California Constitution.
The bond proceeds may only be used for specific projects listed in "Exhibit B" of the Measure. The "Project
List" section of Exhibit B contains the specific projects the District proposes to finance with proceeds from
the sale of the bonds authorized by this ballot Measure. These projects include: construction of a new
replacement inpatient acute care wing and emergency department to the San Bernardino Mountains
Community Hospital (Hospital) building; a new entry, drop-off and pick-up area to improve patient access
to the Hospital; upgrades and renovation of the existing inpatient acute care unit for use as long term care
beds; infrastructure, utility and systems upgrades; grading and other construction and improvements to the
existing receiving and parking areas located adjacent to the Hospital's main entrance and medical office
building to improve patient access to the Hospital; and seismic upgrades to keep patients, skilled nursing
care residents, doctors, nurses and other employees and visitors safe at the Hospital and to keep the
hospital fully functional with temporary power, water and wastewater systems for up to 72 hours if cut off
from external utilities during emergency situations.
The District will conduct financial and performance audits annually and all bond expenditures will be
monitored by an independent Citizens' Oversight Committee to review the use of bond revenues and
determine whether the bond funds were used for proper purposes and review annual financial audits. The
District will also create an account into which proceeds of the bonds shall be deposited and comply with
the reporting requirements of Government Code section 53410 and prepare annual reports to comply with
the requirements of Government Code section 53411.
The bonded debt will be a general obligation of the District and will be financed by taxes levied annually on
taxable property located within the District.
